use @piton/belay
from ../Factory import Factory
from ./lib/FactorySkillBase import FactorySkillBase

export skill Factory0010Scaffold extends FactorySkillBase:
    description: Scaffold a project's initial factory from the tethered specification.
    useWhen: starting a project factory implementation where no factory exists yet
    prompt:
        Read @{Factory.scaffoldCapability}, @{Factory.scaffoldOutput},
        @{Factory.standardSurface}, and @{Factory.commandInvocation}. Inspect
        the project's specification, technology preferences, and existing tools
        before creating files. If a factory implementation already exists,
        report it and use onboarding or enhancement instead of scaffolding over
        it.

        Interview only for the choices scaffolding needs, one clear question at
        a time, such as the implementation technology and where configuration
        and the entry point should live. Do not invent unresolved command
        behavior, schemas, or defaults. Keep project-specific decisions in
        project-owned Piton or configuration.

        Create a runnable minimal core in the chosen technology. Include the
        descriptor, a configuration file, all six core commands (onboard, start,
        submit, status, answer, shutdown), and a minimal dashboard that shows
        the monitored facts and exposes the core actions. Omit optional commands
        unless the engineer asks for them. Do not scaffold tests the engineer
        did not request, and do not implement the full capability catalog.

        Follow the descriptor schema exactly, including schemaVersion,
        invocation, config, specVersion, and builtAgainstSpecVersion. Validate
        the surface in an isolated sandbox with stubbed external services and a
        cost cap. Stage writes and write the descriptor last; never leave a
        half-scaffold that blocks retry. Report commands you could not verify as
        unverified, not working.

        Record how the factory is invoked in the fixed project descriptor,
        together with the configuration location, the tethered specification
        version, and the dashboard location when applicable. State which
        optional commands the implementation supports. Do not depend on or fork
        the reference implementation. Preserve existing user work and do not
        overwrite files without permission.

        Validate the result through the recorded entry point. Demonstrate that
        the six commands and the dashboard respond, and that configuration is
        read from its actual location. Do not claim the factory is operational
        when only files exist or when external services remain unconfigured.
        Report unresolved decisions rather than filling them silently.
    result:
        Report the technology chosen, files created, how invocation is
        recorded, which commands and dashboard behavior were verified, and
        which decisions remain open.
